Hello,

Thank you very much for the quick reply!

I appreciate the additional information regarding the parameterization of the negative binomial model in R-INLA and how I might approach alternative specifications. In regard to your second response, this is right along the lines of what I was thinking, especially with regards to specifying the poisson model with a unit-level random effect to account for the over-dispersion.

Thank you!
Nick

On Tuesday, July 27, 2021 at 10:02:43 AM UTC+1 he...@r-inla.org wrote:
> On Mon, 2021-07-26 at 13:14 -0700, Nicholas Bakewell wrote:
> >
> > I have count data that may normally be modelled assuming a Poisson
> > distribution, but due to the presence of over-dispersion,
>
> you may equally well, just use a Gaussian random effect with the
> poisson, like (idx=1:n)
>
> y ~ ... + f(idx,model="iid") + ...
>
> and family="poisson", instead of
>
> y ~ ...
>
> and family="nbinomial"
>
> this is more or less the same, as nbinom is with f() is log of a Gamma
> with mean 1.
>
> the offset should be related to how the data are recorded, if its
> counts, then the offset is log(number of patients) and you have accout
> for the /1000 later.
>
> y ~ offset(log(n.pat)) + ...
>
> and poisson, is the same as
>
> y ~ ....
>
> and poisson and E=n.pat
>
>
>
>
>
>
>
>
> > I've opted for a negative binomial distribution, with dispersion
> > parameterized in terms of the mean accounting for an offset. My data is
> > hierarchical in nature with 5 repeated annual measurements of
> > prescription rates recorded for GP offices nested within higher-level
> > health authorities (i.e., a 3-level model). Thus, I'd like to include
> > random effects (intercepts) for the GP offices and higher-level health
> > authorities; I will add a spatially structured component later on at the
> > health authority level. I am interested in modelling the prescribing
> > rate per 1000 patients, so my offset is log(number of patients/1000).
> > However, from reading the nbinomial R-INLA documentation, perhaps I
> > should specify the exposure E as (number of patients/1000) and variant=1
> > in the control.family argument to get dispersion modelled in terms of
> > the mean accounting for a different offset i for every observation as
> > Stata does, and not assuming constant over-dispersion as it seems
> > variant=0 does. That said, it seems I shouldn't have offset=log(number
> > of patients/1000), but instead use the E argument. Please see some
> > sample code below, with no adjustment to priors, so I wouldn't expect
> > the estimates to be so different (the factor variable and the intercept
> > are where the estimates differ the most):
> >
> > formula.modeMLM = NumItems ~ 1  + CPercHyp + CPercOB + yrc + yrc2 +
> >        CGPHeadcount + factor(IMDScoreQuintile_LOCF) +
> >                     f(CCG_ID, model="iid") + #Health Authority/CCG
> >                     f(CCG_ORG, model="iid") #GP office/practice
> >
> > resultsMLM = inla(formula.modeMLM, family="nbinomial", data=MLM ,
> > E=patsper1000,
> >  control.family=list(link='log', variant=1),
> >                            control.compute=list(dic=TRUE, cpo=TRUE,
> > waic=TRUE))
> >
> > Is there anything that just seems wrong in my specification leading to
> > different estimates, or is Stata's specification just not possible in R-
> > INLA, thus, I can simply say they differ because they are not exactly
> > the same parameterization (see attached Stata's write-up on how they
> > parameterize over-dispersion)? Happy to share data.
> >
> > Thank you for reading my post and any help is appreciated!
> > Nick
> >
> >
